CHAPTERS=[
('1 · A queue on Platform 1','A passenger boards without finding an accessible, clean station toilet. Should this be treated only as a station engineering issue?'),
('2 · The geometry of convenience','600 m platform; walking time, gender mix and bursty arrivals determine toilet distribution and WC capacity.'),
('3 · Water behind the WC','A toilet needs water, GLR, elevated storage, pumping, power, sewage treatment and reuse.'),
('4 · The human system','Fixtures do not clean themselves. Model shifts, relief staff, inspections, consumables and uptime.'),
('5 · Impact beyond the station','Convenient station toilets may displace onboard visits and improve coach cleanliness; test causally.'),
('6 · A price at the toilet door','Fees recover some expenditure yet can discourage use, induce collection costs, and shift demand to coaches.'),
('7 · What should the ticket buy?','Basic clean sanitation can be funded through general passenger-service revenue and station budgets, not necessarily a new surcharge.'),
('8 · Let the policy compete','Compare free basic, WC fee, free basic plus optional premium, and targeted exemptions against welfare and budget constraints.'),
('9 · AI changes the decision process','A model is a testable hypothesis; deploy instruments, gather real demand, calibrate, compare and revise.'),
('10 · The management decision','Approve a performance-based pilot with transparent funding, sanitation SLAs and measurable coach-cleanliness outcomes.')]
